.TH REGION 8
.SH NAME
region \- the radio region: which country's Sub-GHz rules apply
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBregion\fR
\fBregion list\fR
\fBregion show\fR [\fICC\fR]
\fBregion check\fR \fICC MHZ\fR [\fB\-p\fR \fIpreset\fR] [\fB\-P\fR \fIdbm\fR] [\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-a\fR \fIms\fR]
\fBregion set\fR [\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] \fICC\fR
\fBregion compile\fR \fICC file\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every Sub-GHz transmit is checked against the rules of one country, the
radio region (Galtzagorriak MINIX, spec section Q).
.I CC
is its ISO 3166 two-letter code.
The tables are text files,
.BI /usr/share/region/ CC .reg ,
one row per band with its power, duty cycle, bandwidth, kind of use and
the legal source.
.PP
Without arguments,
.B region
prints the code in
.B /etc/flipper/region
and the one the kernel uses.
.B list
prints the countries with a table,
.B show
a country's table (default: the region set): the band, the power limit,
the CC1101 step used (PA), the share of any hour that may be sent, the
largest bandwidth and the kind of use.
Rows marked
.B ?
are not checked against the national text yet and are never used.
.PP
.B check
says whether one transmit at
.I MHZ
would be allowed, and in which row; it never sends.
.B \-p
is a
.BR gmodem (1)
preset (default std),
.B \-P
the power in dBm (default \-30),
.B \-c
a remote's code instead of data,
.B \-a
the airtime in ms (default 1000).
.PP
.B set
(root only) checks the table (and its
.B Base:
file), prints it, compiles it into the region record, checks the
files' SHA-256 against the list of the tables shipped with this firmware,
asks "I will use this device under the rules of CC" (\fB\-y\fR answers
yes), writes the code to
.B /etc/flipper/region
and logs the change, with the hashes, to
.BR /usr/adm/messages .
.B /etc/rc
gives the code to the kernel at the next boot.
A code without a table is refused: transmit stays off.
.PP
A file whose hash is not in the list is a
.IR "custom table" :
.B set
says so, prints its hash and the shipped one of the same code, and
refuses unless
.B \-f
is given.
You are responsible for a custom table; the log line says
.B custom
and gives its hash.
.PP
The record (16 bytes, plus 16 for each row not marked
.BR ? ,
at most 528 bytes) carries the magic RGN1, the code, the row count and
a CRC-32; \fBset\fR prints its size and CRC.
.B compile
writes it to
.I file
(any user; for tests and tools).
The flash copy of the record, written once per change of region so the
limits hold without the card, comes with the installed-region record of
task T32b; until then the kernel keeps its own band set per country.
.SH FILES
.TP 25
.B /usr/share/region/*.reg
the tables (base package)
.TP
.B /etc/flipper/region
the code set
.TP
.B /usr/adm/messages
the log of every \fBregion set\fR
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR gmodem (1),
.BR subghz (1).
.SH NOTES
You are responsible for using the radio legally where you are: when you
travel, set the region of the country you are in.
The tables follow the law, not the stock firmware's region data, which
allows more.
Tables exist for the 27 EU member states (each is the EU table plus its
own unchecked 870/915 MHz rows), EU, NO, GB, AL, BA, MK, US and CA.
